// EXPECT_COMPILE_ERROR E7330
//
// D27-amendment ([221.1 #24], 2026-07-23): `[](str, str)` WITHOUT a
// trailing type-usage continuation (`.`/`{`) is NOT reinterpreted as a
// type — it stays the ORIGINAL empty-literal-then-call parse, which is
// always a compile error (calling a value literal is never legal). This
// negative fixture pins down that the amendment does not lose the
// existing diagnostic for a genuinely malformed call: `str`/`str` here
// are read as bare VALUE arguments to a call on `[]` (the empty literal),
// and the checker still reports `E7330` ("str is a record type, not a
// value") on them, exactly as it did before the amendment.

module neg.array_type_expr_position_neg

fn bad() -> () {
    ro y = [](str, str)
    ()
}
